      <div class="article-body blog-article-content">
        <h2 id="what-is">What Is MALAI VIZHI?</h2>
        <p>
          MALAI VIZHI is a location-based landslide risk monitoring and early-warning platform designed for the North Eastern Region (NER) of India. The platform combines multiple environmental data sources — rainfall, soil moisture, terrain slope, historical landslide data, and citizen-submitted observations — into a unified risk assessment that can be understood by both technical users and community members.
        </p>
        <p>
          The system is not a single sensor. It is not a weather forecast service. And it is not an artificial intelligence system that claims to predict when a landslide will occur. It is a system that aggregates, normalizes, and presents environmental evidence in a way that supports informed risk awareness and earlier preparedness — before environmental conditions become dangerous.
        </p>

        <h2 id="context">The North Eastern Region India Context</h2>
        <p>
          The North Eastern Region of India comprises eight states: Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim, and Tripura. Geographically, much of this region sits within mountainous terrain — the eastern Himalayas, the Shillong Plateau, the Naga Hills, the Mizo Hills, and the Barail Range.
        </p>
        <p>
          These landscapes are characterized by steep slopes, dense forest cover, high annual rainfall (some of the highest in the world, including Cherrapunji and Mawsynram in Meghalaya), and a complex pattern of monsoon precipitation. The combination of these factors makes the region inherently susceptible to landslides during and after heavy rainfall events.
        </p>
        <p>
          Communities, roads, bridges, hospitals, and other critical infrastructure exist in this terrain. The ability to understand when environmental conditions are shifting toward more hazardous configurations — even imperfectly — provides time for awareness, preparedness, and response.
        </p>

        <h2 id="problem">The Landslide Problem</h2>
        <p>
          A landslide does not occur because of rainfall alone. The combination of factors that leads to a slope failure involves terrain angle, soil type, vegetation cover, accumulated moisture in the soil column, intensity and duration of rainfall, and the presence of existing instability zones.
        </p>
        <p>
          This means that a monitoring system based on a single signal — for example, "it is raining heavily, so there is risk" — will have limited accuracy and limited usefulness. Sometimes heavy rainfall occurs on terrain where the slope is gentle and the soil drains quickly — landslide risk remains low. Sometimes moderate rainfall occurs on a steep slope where the soil is already saturated from previous weeks — risk can be significantly elevated.
        </p>
        <p>
          MALAI VIZHI is designed to address this fundamental challenge by combining multiple signals rather than relying on any single measurement.
        </p>

        <h2 id="purpose">The Purpose of the System</h2>
        <p>
          The core purpose of MALAI VIZHI is to transform raw environmental data into an understandable and explainable risk indication for a specific geographic location — and to deliver that indication to the people who need it, in a form they can use.
        </p>
        <p>
          This involves:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Collecting</strong> environmental data from available sources (Open-Meteo for rainfall and soil moisture, Copernicus DEM for terrain, historical databases for susceptibility context)</li>
          <li><strong>Processing</strong> that data to validate, normalize, and assess its quality</li>
          <li><strong>Assessing</strong> the combined environmental conditions to produce a risk score and risk level classification</li>
          <li><strong>Warning</strong> configured recipients when risk exceeds configured thresholds</li>
          <li><strong>Supporting</strong> authority response with contextual information, GIS visualization, and citizen report evidence</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="not-about">What MALAI VIZHI Is Not</h2>
        <p>
          Clarity about limitations is as important as clarity about capabilities. MALAI VIZHI is <strong>not</strong> the following:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Not a system that guarantees prediction of when a landslide will occur</li>
          <li>Not a nationwide physical sensor network — it is designed for ground sensor integration but sensors are not currently deployed at scale</li>
          <li>Not an AI system using neural networks or trained machine learning models for risk computation</li>
          <li>Not a system that fabricates values when data is unavailable — missing data is shown as UNAVAILABLE with appropriate confidence reduction</li>
          <li>Not a replacement for qualified geotechnical assessment, authority verification, or physical ground inspection in critical situations</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="how-name">About the Name: MALAI VIZHI</h2>
        <p>
          The name MALAI VIZHI is derived from Tamil — two words that together form a meaningful name for the system. <em>Malai</em> (மலை) means "mountain" or "hill." <em>Vizhi</em> (விழி) means "eye" or "sight." Together, MALAI VIZHI translates as "mountain sight" or more expressively, "seeing the hill" — which aligns with the project tagline: <strong>Seeing the Risk Before the Landslide.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The name reflects the system's purpose: to observe and understand what is happening in mountainous terrain, to make that information visible, and to provide earlier awareness of conditions that may indicate elevated risk.
        </p>

        <h2 id="beyond">Beyond a Sensor Network</h2>
        <p>
          One of the important distinctions of MALAI VIZHI is that it does not require a complete physical sensor network to provide value. The system is designed to ingest sensor data when sensors are available, but it can also operate with satellite-derived environmental data, DEM-based terrain analysis, and historical susceptibility context — all available without deploying physical hardware.
        </p>
        <p>
          This design decision means that the platform can provide risk context even in regions where physical sensor deployment has not yet occurred — while being designed to leverage sensor data as it is deployed incrementally over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is reflected in the system's confidence scoring: a risk assessment made without ground sensor data carries lower confidence than one supported by direct telemetry. The system communicates this honestly — a risk score of 65 with 20% confidence (LIMITED) carries different implications than the same risk score with 80% confidence (HIGH).
        </p>

